Well, it just means that if I wanted to talk about a god who existed
whether or not ey was worshipped I'd need to use a zi'evla or {cevni be
zi'o}. A priest might call the Christian god {lo cevni be zi'o bei roda}.

Even if you don't like philosophy, this is still a linguistic issue. In
English, the Goa'uld are *not* gods, even though they are worshiped as
gods; in lojban (assuming your definition as canonical, which makes sense)
they *are* gods *because* they are worshiped as such.


